Resolver: fix off-by-one read in ngx_resolver_copy() for SRV records.

Backport of upstream nginx edb4d2ffa746ba30bcae90e9527d5512b3de2c5b
("Resolver: fixed off-by-one read in ngx_resolver_copy()", first released in
nginx 1.29.6 / stable-1.28.3; cherry-pick 067c0b3f5 on stable-1.28):
https://github.com/nginx/nginx/commit/edb4d2ffa746ba30bcae90e9527d5512b3de2c5b

In ngx_resolver_process_srv(), the length check before ngx_resolver_copy() of
an SRV record's target uses "i + 6 > n", so a record whose 6 bytes of fixed
fields end exactly at the end of the message passes the check, and
ngx_resolver_copy() then reads buf[n] (one byte past the message) before its
own bounds check. Upstream changed the guard to "i + 6 >= n". Upstream believes
it harmless (it only affects control flow; see the analogous 077a890a76ff), and
it is not reachable in the configuration this image builds: nginx 1.27.1 has no
"server ... resolve service=" (added in 1.27.3) and none of the bundled modules
set ctx->service, so no SRV lookup is started. Backported for parity with the
upstream resolver so a future "service=" user is not exposed.

Compatibility: src/core/ngx_resolver.c only; no public header, struct, symbol or
directive change, so no ABI impact for third-party modules.

--- a/src/core/ngx_resolver.c
+++ b/src/core/ngx_resolver.c
@@ -3024,7 +3024,7 @@ ngx_resolver_process_srv(ngx_resolver_t
 
         case NGX_RESOLVE_SRV:
 
-            if (i + 6 > n) {
+            if (i + 6 >= n) {
                 goto short_response;
             }
 
